export const synthesis =
  'Chapter 8 explains why practical Kohn–Sham DFT can work despite the unknown exact exchange–correlation functional. The exchange–correlation hole provides the physical object that connects Pauli exclusion, Coulomb correlation, self-interaction cancellation, and the coupling-constant average to the energy. LSDA replaces that hole locally by the one from a homogeneous electron gas, while GGA adds dimensionless information about density variation and enforces selected constraints when a naive gradient expansion fails. The success of these approximations depends partly on integrated and spherical-average properties of the hole, which are less demanding than reproducing its full shape, but their failures become visible in self-interaction, asymptotic potentials, stretched bonds, strongly nonlocal correlation, and large-gradient regimes. The functional derivative then introduces a second layer of responsibility because a useful energy functional must also generate a stable and physically meaningful Kohn–Sham potential. Chapter 9 continues from this point by allowing orbitals, kinetic-energy density, explicit nonlocality, and generalized Kohn–Sham operators to carry information that a semilocal density functional cannot represent.';
